Id: logf008w
Severity: WARN
Category: logging
Check: check_audit
Fix: Set Storage=persistent in journald.conf (or mkdir /var/log/journal) and restart journald.
References: journald.conf(5)
Controls: CIS 8.2, 8.5; NIST AU-2, AU-12; ISO 8.15

The system's logs are not kept across a reboot. journald keeps them in
memory (/run/log/journal) when Storage= is volatile or none, or when it
is auto (the default) and /var/log/journal does not exist, and no
syslog daemon (rsyslog, syslog-ng) is running to write them to disk.
A reboot, including the one that follows a crash or an intrusion,
erases everything that led up to it.

To fix it, set Storage=persistent in /etc/systemd/journald.conf (or in
a drop-in under /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/), or create
/var/log/journal, then 'systemctl restart systemd-journald'. Set
SystemMaxUse= there too if disk space is tight.
